(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physiology-exam-3-metabolism-quiz-version-5"]={"title":"Metabolism, Nutrition & Thermoregulation — Quiz 4 (30 Questions)","category":"Physiology","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"After muscle produces lactate during intense exercise (to regenerate NAD+ for continued glycolysis), that lactate is transported to the liver, where it is...","choices":["immediately converted into acetyl-CoA and fully oxidized in the liver","converted directly into glycogen without first forming pyruvate or glucose","excreted directly and completely unchanged in bile","converted back to pyruvate and then to glucose via gluconeogenesis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The liver converts lactate back to pyruvate (a step requiring NAD+, which the liver has in adequate supply unlike the exercising muscle) and then uses gluconeogenesis to regenerate glucose, which can be released back into the blood for use by muscle and other tissues. — Chapter 68 (Carbohydrate Metabolism), Slide 25"},{"q":"Lactate can be thought of as a temporary 'storage form' of NADH because...","choices":["converting pyruvate to lactate consumes NADH, and converting lactate back to pyruvate regenerates it","lactate formation requires no NADH or NAD+ involvement of any kind whatsoever","lactate directly contains intact NADH molecules physically bound within its own structure","lactate is chemically identical in structure to the NADH molecule itself"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. When pyruvate is reduced to lactate, NADH is consumed (oxidized to NAD+); when lactate is later converted back to pyruvate (e.g., in the liver), NAD+ is reduced back to NADH — so the reducing equivalents originally carried by NADH are effectively preserved in lactate until it's reconverted. — Chapter 68 (Carbohydrate Metabolism), Slide 25"},{"q":"Beyond converting lactate back to glucose, the oxygen debt repaid after strenuous exercise is also used to help re-establish which of the following?","choices":["Further depletion of the body's remaining glycogen stores","Normal blood pH only, with no effect on oxygen-carrying molecules","Elevated core body temperature exclusively, with no other change","Normal oxy-hemoglobin/myoglobin status and normal lung oxygen levels"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In addition to using oxygen to convert lactate back to glucose and regenerate ATP/phosphocreatine, the oxygen debt also covers re-establishing normal oxy-hemoglobin and oxy-myoglobin saturation and restoring normal oxygen levels within the lungs themselves. — Chapter 73 (Energetics), Slide 6"},{"q":"The reciprocal 'glucose-fatty acid cycle' reflects the fact that the glycerol needed to re-esterify fatty acids into triglycerides in adipose tissue must come specifically from...","choices":["dietary cholesterol freshly absorbed from the small intestine lumen directly","glycolysis (glucose metabolism), since the glycerol released by lipolysis cannot be directly reused","direct reuse of the glycerol released by the adipocyte's own lipolysis","beta-oxidation of those very same fatty acids currently being re-esterified"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Adipose tissue cannot simply reuse the glycerol released by its own ongoing lipolysis to resynthesize triglycerides; the glycerol needed for re-esterification must instead be supplied via glycolysis, which is why active glucose utilization for energy tends to promote fat storage, and vice versa. — Chapter 73 (Energetics), Slide 13"},{"q":"Folic acid (Vitamin B9), described as slightly water-soluble and stored in the liver, is best obtained from food sources such as...","choices":["dairy products exclusively","red meat exclusively, with no plant sources","citrus fruits exclusively","kidney, liver, and dark green vegetables"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Folic acid's best dietary sources include organ meats such as kidney and liver, as well as dark green vegetables, and unlike most water-soluble vitamins, a meaningful amount is stored in the liver. — Chapter 72 (Dietary Balances/Vitamins), Slide 25"},{"q":"Beyond short-term signals like leptin and ghrelin, intermediate and long-term regulation of appetite is also influenced by the circulating availability of...","choices":["only ambient environmental temperature","carbohydrate, lipid, and protein themselves","only renal urine osmolarity levels","only circulating red blood cell count"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Beyond hormonal signals, the hypothalamus also senses the circulating availability of the macronutrients themselves — carbohydrate, lipid, and protein — which contributes to intermediate and long-term regulation of feeding behavior. — Chapter 72 (Dietary Balances/Vitamins), Slide 13"},{"q":"The functional unit of the liver, within which hepatocytes are arranged around a central vein and blood flows inward from portal triads, is called the...","choices":["nephron","alveolus","liver lobule","acinus of the pancreas"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The liver lobule is the classic functional and structural unit of the liver, organized with hepatocyte plates radiating around a central vein while blood flows in from the portal triads (branches of the portal vein and hepatic artery) at the lobule's periphery. — Chapter 71 (The Liver), Slide 4"},{"q":"Immediately after a carbohydrate-rich meal, when the body is burning mainly glucose, the respiratory quotient (RQ) approaches...","choices":["1.5","0.3","1.0","0.7"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. When carbohydrate is the dominant fuel being oxidized, CO2 production closely matches O2 consumption on a molar basis, giving an RQ near 1.0 — higher than the roughly 0.7 seen with pure fat oxidation. — Chapter 72 (Dietary Balances/Vitamins), Slide 6"},{"q":"The liver's central role as a 'glucose buffer' means that, between meals, it helps keep blood glucose from falling chiefly through...","choices":["secreting insulin directly into the hepatic portal vein itself","actively absorbing more glucose from the intestine between meals","converting all remaining glucose into ketone bodies immediately","glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis, releasing glucose into the blood as needed"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Acting as a glucose buffer, the liver releases stored glucose via glycogenolysis and, once glycogen is exhausted, generates new glucose via gluconeogenesis, both of which help stabilize blood glucose between meals when dietary intake has stopped. — Chapter 71 (The Liver), Slide 8"},{"q":"Among the three macronutrients, protein produces the greatest thermic effect (rise in metabolic rate during digestion/processing), largely because of the extra energy required for...","choices":["conversion of amino acids directly into ketone bodies","bile salt recycling triggered specifically by protein","deamination and processing of amino acids, including urea synthesis","simple passive diffusion of amino acids across the intestinal wall"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Protein produces the largest thermic effect of any macronutrient because processing absorbed amino acids — including deamination and the energy-requiring synthesis of urea to dispose of the resulting nitrogen — costs meaningfully more energy than digesting and processing carbohydrate or fat. — Chapter 73 (Energetics), Slide 9"},{"q":"Heat loss from the body by radiation differs from heat loss by conduction in that radiation...","choices":["is identical in underlying physical mechanism to evaporative heat loss","transfers heat as infrared electromagnetic waves without requiring direct physical contact","only ever occurs while the body is fully submerged underwater in cold water","requires direct physical contact with another object, the same as conduction"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Radiant heat loss occurs via infrared electromagnetic waves traveling from the warmer body surface to cooler surrounding objects, requiring no direct physical contact — unlike conduction, which requires direct contact between the skin and another surface or medium. — Chapter 74 (Body Temperature), Slide 7"},{"q":"Convective heat loss from the body is distinguished from simple conduction by the fact that convection specifically involves...","choices":["conversion of surface sweat directly into water vapor released at the skin surface","movement of air (or another fluid) that continuously carries heated air away and replaces it with cooler air","no involvement of any air or fluid movement whatsoever in the underlying process at all","direct transfer of heat only through a completely stationary, unmoving medium"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Convective heat loss depends on air (or fluid) movement — as warmed air near the skin is carried away and replaced by cooler air, heat loss continues; this is why wind or fanning increases convective heat loss compared with still air. — Chapter 74 (Body Temperature), Slide 7"},{"q":"Beta-oxidation of fatty acids occurs within the...","choices":["mitochondria","cytoplasm exclusively","Golgi apparatus","nucleus"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Beta-oxidation, the process that sequentially breaks fatty acid chains down into two-carbon acetyl-CoA units, takes place within the mitochondria, where those acetyl-CoA units can then enter the citric acid cycle for further energy extraction. — Chapter 69 (Lipid Metabolism), Slide 15"},{"q":"The amino acid pool that supplies the body's ongoing protein synthesis needs is best described as...","choices":["located exclusively within circulating red blood cells and found nowhere else in the entire body","a single, large, permanently fixed store of amino acids, closely analogous to the body's fat reserves","entirely replenished only once per day, exclusively at a single fixed daily mealtime","a small, dynamically turning-over reserve of free amino acids, continuously replenished by protein turnover and diet"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Unlike the large stored reserves of fat or glycogen, the body's free amino acid pool is relatively small at any given moment, but it is continuously and dynamically replenished by dietary intake and the ongoing breakdown of body proteins, supplying the raw material for new protein synthesis. — Chapter 70 (Protein Metabolism), Slide 6"},{"q":"When comparing energy content per gram, dietary carbohydrate and protein each provide approximately how much energy, compared with fat's approximately 9 kcal/g?","choices":["About 2 kcal/g each","About 9 kcal/g each, identical to fat","About 4 kcal/g each","About 7 kcal/g each"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Both carbohydrate and protein provide approximately 4 kcal/g, while fat provides substantially more energy per gram (about 9 kcal/g) — more than double — which is why fat serves as such an efficient, compact long-term energy store. — Chapter 72 (Dietary Balances/Vitamins), Slide 4"},{"q":"Lipoprotein lipase, anchored to the capillary endothelium of tissues like adipose and muscle, hydrolyzes triglyceride carried within circulating lipoproteins into...","choices":["free fatty acids and glycerol, which can then enter the underlying tissue","cholesterol esters exclusively, leaving triglyceride untouched","free circulating glucose molecules and amino acids directly","ketone bodies directly, bypassing triglyceride entirely"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Lipoprotein lipase hydrolyzes the triglyceride core of circulating chylomicrons and VLDL into free fatty acids and glycerol at the capillary wall, allowing the fatty acids to diffuse into adjacent adipose or muscle cells for storage or use as fuel. — Chapter 69 (Lipid Metabolism), Slide 11"},{"q":"Growth hormone promotes a positive nitrogen balance (net protein gain) largely by...","choices":["completely blocking all amino acid uptake into cells throughout the entire body indefinitely","increasing tissue protein breakdown specifically to fuel ongoing hepatic gluconeogenesis","stimulating hepatic ketone body production as its single primary mechanism of action overall","increasing amino acid uptake into cells and stimulating protein synthesis while sparing protein from being used for energy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Growth hormone promotes tissue growth by increasing cellular amino acid uptake and stimulating protein synthesis, while also shifting the body toward using fat rather than protein for energy — together producing a positive nitrogen balance (more nitrogen retained in protein than excreted). — Chapter 70 (Protein Metabolism), Slide 19"},{"q":"Cortisol's influence on protein metabolism is largely opposite to growth hormone's, in that cortisol tends to...","choices":["exclusively affect fat metabolism, sparing tissue protein entirely from breakdown","increase breakdown of tissue protein, mobilizing amino acids for gluconeogenesis","have no measurable effect on tissue protein metabolism whatsoever","increase protein synthesis in a manner identical to growth hormone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Cortisol promotes a catabolic state for protein — increasing breakdown of tissue (especially muscle) protein and mobilizing the resulting amino acids toward the liver for gluconeogenesis — the opposite of growth hormone's protein-sparing, anabolic effect. — Chapter 70 (Protein Metabolism), Slide 19"},{"q":"In a sedentary person, the single largest component of total daily energy expenditure is typically...","choices":["the thermic effect of food","shivering thermogenesis","basal metabolic rate","physical activity"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. For a sedentary individual, basal metabolic rate (the energy required for essential resting bodily functions) makes up the largest share of total daily energy expenditure, exceeding the contributions of physical activity or the thermic effect of food. — Chapter 73 (Energetics), Slide 10"},{"q":"Larger individuals tend to have a higher total basal metabolic rate partly because...","choices":["basal metabolic rate is completely independent of body size or overall lean tissue mass","they have proportionally more body fat, which is itself a highly metabolically active tissue","their cells individually consume disproportionately more energy at rest, unrelated to body size","they have greater total body surface area and lean tissue mass, both of which raise resting energy expenditure"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A larger body generally has both greater body surface area (over which heat is lost, requiring more heat production to maintain temperature) and more metabolically active lean tissue mass, both of which raise total basal metabolic rate compared with a smaller individual. — Chapter 73 (Energetics), Slide 10"},{"q":"With repeated exposure to a hot environment, acclimatization improves heat tolerance largely by enhancing the...","choices":["capacity to shiver more vigorously in the cold","sensitivity of cold receptors located in the skin","thickness of the subcutaneous fat layer overall","capacity and responsiveness of the sweating mechanism"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Heat acclimatization primarily works by enhancing the sweat glands' capacity and responsiveness — allowing greater sweat output to begin sooner and more efficiently — improving evaporative heat loss and overall heat tolerance with repeated hot-environment exposure. — Chapter 74 (Body Temperature), Slide 11"},{"q":"When a fever finally 'breaks' and resolves, a person often flushes and sweats profusely because the hypothalamic set point has...","choices":["been disabled entirely, eliminating any further temperature regulation","shifted temperature control entirely from the hypothalamus to the spinal cord","returned to normal, so the now-relatively-too-warm body triggers heat-loss mechanisms","been permanently raised even higher than during the fever itself"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. As the hypothalamic set point drops back down to its normal value, the body (whose actual temperature is still elevated relative to the new, lower set point) responds as if it were too warm, triggering vasodilation and sweating to rapidly dissipate the excess heat — producing the characteristic flushing and sweating as a fever breaks. — Chapter 74 (Body Temperature), Slide 17"},{"q":"Sweat reaching the skin surface is normally hypotonic (less concentrated) relative to plasma because...","choices":["sweat gland ducts reabsorb sodium and chloride from the fluid as it travels to the surface, especially when flow is slow","sweat glands actively secrete pure water with absolutely no dissolved ions at all from the very start","sweat composition is entirely and fundamentally unrelated to plasma composition or ion content","sweat becomes progressively more concentrated, not less, than plasma as it travels toward the surface"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Although the fluid initially secreted by sweat glands resembles plasma in composition, the duct reabsorbs sodium and chloride (a process aided by aldosterone) as the fluid moves toward the skin surface; at slower flow rates, there is more time for this reabsorption, producing a final hypotonic sweat, especially important for sodium conservation. — Chapter 74 (Body Temperature), Slide 8"},{"q":"Basal metabolic rate (BMR) is measured under specific standardized conditions, including that the subject be...","choices":["awake, at physical and mental rest, in a thermoneutral environment, and at least 12 hours after the last meal (postabsorptive state)","fully asleep, resting within a cold, thermally stressful environment that requires active heat production","tested immediately after eating a large, calorie-dense meal, while still in the active digestive state","actively exercising at maximum physical exertion on a treadmill or stationary cycle ergometer device"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. BMR is measured under carefully standardized conditions — the subject awake but resting physically and mentally, in a thermoneutral environment, and in the postabsorptive state (at least 12 hours fasted) — to eliminate the metabolic contributions of muscular activity, thermoregulation, and the thermic effect of food. — Chapter 73 (Energetics), Slide 8"},{"q":"Indirect calorimetry estimates whole-body metabolic rate primarily by measuring...","choices":["heart rate alone exclusively","total circulating blood volume","core body temperature alone","the rate of oxygen consumption"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Indirect calorimetry estimates metabolic rate by measuring the rate of oxygen consumption (and often CO2 production, to calculate RQ), using the known energy yield per liter of oxygen consumed across different fuel mixes to back-calculate overall energy expenditure. — Chapter 73 (Energetics), Slide 7"},{"q":"To conserve heat when exposed to cold, sympathetic signals cause cutaneous (skin) blood vessels to...","choices":["remain completely unaffected by sympathetic input regardless of temperature","dilate substantially, increasing blood flow to the skin in order to conserve heat","constrict, reducing blood flow to the skin and minimizing heat loss to the environment","dilate only in the extremities while constricting everywhere else"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Cold exposure triggers sympathetically mediated cutaneous vasoconstriction, which reduces blood flow (and therefore heat delivery) to the skin surface, minimizing radiant/convective/conductive heat loss to the cooler environment and helping conserve core body heat. — Chapter 74 (Body Temperature), Slide 15"},{"q":"Shivering raises body temperature primarily by...","choices":["involuntary, rhythmic skeletal muscle contractions that generate heat as a byproduct of muscle work","directly increasing circulating thyroid hormone secretion within a matter of seconds","reducing overall skeletal muscle activity in order to conserve metabolic energy","increasing eccrine sweat gland activity broadly across the entire skin surface"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Shivering is a hypothalamically triggered, involuntary rhythmic contraction of skeletal muscle; because muscle contraction is metabolically inefficient, a large fraction of the energy used is released as heat, providing a rapid way to raise body temperature in the cold. — Chapter 74 (Body Temperature), Slide 16"},{"q":"Physical activity raises total daily energy expenditure primarily because contracting skeletal muscle...","choices":["has no measurable effect whatsoever on whole-body daily energy expenditure","relies entirely on stored body fat, with no accompanying change in ATP turnover","stops consuming any ATP whatsoever during the contraction process itself","consumes substantially more ATP than at rest, requiring increased fuel oxidation to regenerate it"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Muscle contraction during physical activity dramatically increases the rate of ATP consumption compared with resting muscle, which in turn requires a correspondingly higher rate of fuel oxidation (and thus energy expenditure) to keep regenerating that ATP. — Chapter 73 (Energetics), Slide 10"},{"q":"During a sudden fight-or-flight response, epinephrine helps rapidly supply energy to tissues largely by...","choices":["promoting lipogenesis in adipose tissue to build long-term fat energy reserves","stimulating glycogenolysis in liver and muscle, releasing glucose for immediate use","triggering insulin release from the pancreas so tissues rapidly store incoming glucose","activating lipoprotein lipase in adipose tissue to accelerate fat storage there"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Epinephrine released during acute stress rapidly stimulates glycogenolysis in both liver (raising blood glucose for release into circulation) and muscle (providing glucose-6-phosphate directly for local use), quickly mobilizing stored fuel to meet the sudden energy demand of a fight-or-flight response. — Chapter 73 (Energetics), Slide 4"},{"q":"Cells take up cholesterol-rich LDL particles from the blood mainly through...","choices":["phagocytosis performed by every cell type in the body","receptor-mediated endocytosis via LDL receptors","active transport pumps specific to cholesterol ions","simple diffusion directly across the plasma membrane"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Cells express LDL receptors on their surface that specifically bind LDL particles, triggering receptor-mediated endocytosis to internalize the particle and deliver its cholesterol cargo into the cell. — Chapter 69 (Lipid Metabolism), Slide 27"}]};
